14
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:25,360
Big fan of the YouTube channel, big fan of the app and everything you've been doing at Built With Science.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,360
So I'm personally super curious about the growth of the YouTube channel because we are working on YouTube stuff here at Subclub.

16
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:37,600
But I think our audience will be much more interested in the growth of the app.

17
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:43,760
So as a quick summary, Ethan's brother Jeremy started a fitness YouTube channel.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,040
Ethan joined the next year to kind of build the business.

19
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:51,680
And now they've got over seven million subscribers on YouTube.

20
00:01:52,400 --> 00:02:08,240
But I wanted to kind of skip ahead from the early days to when you decided to actually build an app, why you decided to build an app, how big the audience was at that time, and kind of get more into the nuts and bolts of what our audience is going to be most interested in.

21
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,040
We started the channel back in 2018.

22
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:22,000
And throughout that process, we eventually decided to launch these web-based courses, which about 100,000 people joined over the course of the years.

23
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:31,040
And this was just a program that they would take on the web and then there would be spreadsheets that they'd use to track their workouts and nutrition.

24
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:41,360
And the reason we decided to eventually pivot that course into an app was because, one, it was requested by our users a ton.

25
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:50,000
They were tired of using spreadsheets on their phone just to track their workouts and then a separate tool to measure their nutrition.

26
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:51,760
So that was the first reason.

27
00:02:52,320 --> 00:03:04,480
And then secondly, and more importantly, we k needed to pivot in that direction.

28
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,120
So ultimately it came down to those two things.

29
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,120
I mean, it's so cool when when people beg you for a product.

30
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:13,520
I mean, it's a nice position to be in.

31
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:19,120
I mean, so many folks are building apps just, you know, they're trying to create the audience for the app.

32
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,400
It's like you already had the audience and they were begging for an app.

33
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:24,320
So that's such a great place to be.

34
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:26,480
How big was the audience at that point?

35
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:28,240
Like, how big was the channel?

36
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,680
And you said 100,000 people had used a web product.

37
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:35,040
That was at the time you decided to build the app or that was like lifetime.

38
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:43,760
I'm just trying to kind of get a sense for like where where the you know the business and the YouTube channel was at that point where you decided to pivot to an app.

39
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:44,560
Yeah.

40
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:50,960
So at that point I think there was around 30 million or so subscribers and it slowly grew over time.

41
00:03:51,200 --> 00:04:03,200
I think we're growing around a million or so subscribers per year, but at that point we had enough members and the demand was strong enough that they wouldn't stop bothering us when it came to building an app.

42
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:07,520
So yeah, we finally decided to make that as a key, key goal for us.

43
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:08,880
That's really cool.

44
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:23,200
So part of why I wanted to have you on is that I think there is a little bit of a kind of myth that, you know, if you're a big influencer, you've got, you know, three million subscribers, now seven million subscribers, oh, growth is so easy.

45
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:36,080
You just pitch the app at the end of every YouTube video or work it into the video in the middle somewhere and you're just going to get millions of downloads and make hundreds of millions of dollars and everything's gravy.

46
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:37,200
But that's not been the case.

47
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:38,720
You're all doing meta ads.

48
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:47,520
You know, you are growing the app in a more traditional way than I think a lot of people would expect for an influencer built app.

49
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:51,360
Uh, so I'd love for you to kind of talk through some of that with me.

50
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:56,640
Like, you know, even when you launched it with three million subscribers, was it like just a massive spike?

51
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:57,760
That's a great point.

52
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,920
And when we did launch the app, we had hyped it up, obviously to our members and also to just our email lists and publicly.

53
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:09,120
So we did see a spike when we officially launched it.

54
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,360
That was about two years ago.

55
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:21,280
But when it comes to actually marketing the app, like you mentioned, we've been slowly adding on new channels such as meta, such as SEO, uh, AEO.

56
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,920
But for us, the core piece of content has really just been on education and building trust.

57
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,600
It's been less of something that we try and pitch all the time.

58
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,800
It's more of, okay, we're creating this piece of content.

59
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:41,600
How can we position the app so it complements the piece of content that we're putting out?

60
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,600
So it's not necessarily we're thinking about, okay, we have this app.

61
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:49,200
We want to create a piece of content that pitches the app.

62
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:50,880
So it's kind of a different framing.

63
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:54,080
And for us, that trust has been a key mo for us.

64
00:05:54,240 --> 00:06:00,720
And I think with the age of AI, when you can build pretty much anything, that's going to be something we're definitely doubling down on.

65
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:08,960
When you do pitch the app in a video and the video does really well, because, you know, I mean, YouTube, it's like you can get even with seven million subscribers.

66
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:12,960
I haven't looked at Jeremy's channel recently, though, though I have seen a lot of Jeremy's videos.

67
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:21,920
I was telling you before we started recording, you know, I I saw Jeremy's videos way before the app, way before you and I met, like I saw his videos years and years ago.

68
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:30,000
But I would imagine there's, there's like when a video goes viral and like it's tens of millions of views versus like, you know, maybe a million views or whatever.

69
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:32,000
Do you see that in the numbers?

70
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:41,440
Do you see a big spike in revenue is a big spike in downloads or maybe just like, is it more people watch just kind of checking things out where you see a big spike in downloads, but your conversion actually dropped?

71
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:45,200
Kind of curious what the mechanics of that are for a big YouTube channel.

72
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:46,640
Yeah, that's a great question.

73
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,120
And it's definitely what you mentioned.

74
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:55,280
There is a big spike when we do release a video in terms of we'll see a bunch of new trials come in.

75
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:57,920
But what we've noticed is since we track.

76
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:09,360
Pretty much every click coming from YouTube, both on the video and also in our description link, you can actually tell what types of videos lead to a higher trial conversion.

77
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,560
So for example, if it's more of a.

78
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:35,920
of an educational broad type of content where maybe we're sharing a specific diet or a workout program, a program that reaches a higher audience, a larger audience, then that might not necessarily get the same amount of trial activations as to a video that where maybe we're telling a story about someone who started on the app, this is how they transformed their body.

79
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:45,200
So when we create content nowadays, we're really also thinking about how can we tell a story that seamlessly ties into the product pitch again.

80
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:47,360
How does that work as far as evergreen?

81
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:53,920
I mean, you know, I mean., for me again, because I'm kind of a health and fitness nerd and have watched his videos before.

82
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,160
And I think I'm now subscribed even.

83
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,800
His videos are popping up in my feed pretty regularly.

84
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:16,080
Do some of those older videos that perform really well, do they, does it kind of provide a solid base of subscription revenue and installs or is it pretty spiky with like the release of a video really, you see it big time and then it just resets to like a much smaller baseline.

85
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:16,880
Yeah.

86
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,840
So for us it's been pretty baseline.

87
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:30,640
Like we'll see the spikes when the video comes out, but throughout that we've built a prettyetty solid bank of videos where we have a consistent number of trials coming in every single month.

88
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:38,400
And I think with long form content, that's more or less the way things go where people are searching things up using YouTube as their own SEO.

89
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:42,080
And eventually, these older videos are still getting traffic to this day.

90
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:50,320
As a YouTuber, I'm curious whether Jeremy leans on like other YouTubers in the space or other influencers.

91
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:55,360
I imagine he's got friends and probably goes to some of the YouTube events and things like that.

92
00:08:55,600 --> 00:09:00,560
Do you lean on other influencers as well or is it just the built with science?

93
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:01,360
channel.

94
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,400
Right now, we're mainly focused on actually just expanding our internal content team.

95
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:11,040
But Jeremy, I know he does have plans to collab with a few others in the space.

96
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:15,520
I think he's actually going to Texas in about a month to do a big collab.

97
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:16,160
Nice.

98
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:20,880
I mean, I guess I would imagine it's like it feels like every big fitness influencer already has an app.

99
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:28,960
So I guess it's maybe tough from that perspective, like Renaissance Priority, you know, the macro factor.

100
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:33,600
There's a bunch of other apps in the space that are that were built by YouTube influencers.

101
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,640
And one thing I think that Jeremy does differently is.

102
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:51,520
is he'll actually reach out to maybe they're not as known, but he'll reach out to some of the expert researchers and coaches and dieticians to collaborate with for videos in terms of promoting some of the research that they find.

103
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,920
So that's been something that has added a lot of the credibility for the videos.

104
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:56,560
Yeah.

105
00:09:57,040 --> 00:10:03,680
I want to dig into that a little bit more about the like building trust and being more kind of science based approaches and things like that.

106
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:09,840
But you mentioned earlier, you really think a lot about how the app could fit into the content.

107
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:11,680
Dig deeper into that.

108
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:16,800
Like, you know, are you creating a whole video because you think it's a good pitch for the app?

109
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:27,120
And then how do you, you were talking to about, you know, audience trust and audience engagement and things like that where you you want to create a video just because you think it's a great video.

110
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:37,680
Do you sometimes even leave off like you launch videos that don't even mention the app or how do you how do you kind of process all of that balancing the app business with the YouTube business?

111
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:44,560
I think for us, it still comes down to just focusing on core and where we got started.

112
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:48,800
So that's just the content itself and making sure that it's educational.

113
00:10:49,760 --> 00:11:04,480
It's we don't want to sound like a sales pitch on every video and much of it is we're creating this piece of content and okay, maybe at this point in the middle of the video there's a smooth transition, not a hard pitch, a smooth transition.

114
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:15,120
So for most of the time it's it's very soft CTAs throughout the video, but we never really fully pitch a video unless it's maybe something on Instagram, like an Instagram short or something.

115
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:25,360
So then what percentage of his kind of main videos that go out like the full YouTube video have a mention, all of them or is it still a part of percent.

116
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,920
Most of them now will have something in there, either in the middle or near the end.

117
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:36,880
It's got to be tough, balancing all those different factors though, of, of, you know, wanting to maintain the quality in the audience.

118
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:40,480
And then, I mean, do you feel like the audience gets saturated too?

119
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:49,920
I mean, this has been one of the things we've talked about on the podcast before is like, okay, there's seven million people subscribed and he's got plenty of videos that do tens of millions of views.

120
00:11:50,080 --> 00:12:13,840
And I guess that's the thing with the YouTube algorithm now is that it does kind of expand beyond just the subscription base, but does it seem like over time the, the kind of aud peaks are a little lower or like that that the core audience who watches every video like they've already seen it pitched and maybe they they just don't think it's right out for them or are people like getting convinced over time the more times they see it?

121
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:14,480
Yeah.

122
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:20,160
So in terms of saturation, I think it ultimately still comes down to the content.

123
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:25,760
You can have one person watch maybe a diet based video and maybe they're not as interested in it.

124
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:30,400
But then we post maybe another video that's more on the workout side of things and that hooks them in.

125
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:34,000
So I think it really depends on the content that really attracts to them.

126
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:48,480
And we've seen it time again where if we really dig deep into creating an incredible piece of content that will not only increase trials from some of our existing viewers, but also just expand our reach to reaching more and more people.

127
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:56,880
And then once someone sees one video, the algorithm, again, like we'll show a bunch of our other videos, and it kind of creates its own mini funnel inside of YouTube.

128
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:57,840
Right.

129
00:12:58,080 --> 00:12:58,160
Yeah.

130
00:12:58,400 --> 00:12:58,960
That makes sense.

131
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:05,120
And then how do you think about like shorts and reels and TikTok and UGC?

132
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:09,280
Are you working with any like UGC creators?

133
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:12,160
I mean, you mentioned you're not really doing like other influencers.

134
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:21,360
But are you clipping from the main videos to create more kind of ad like UGC?

135
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:22,480
Yeah.

136
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:31,760
So for the most part right now, one thing we've learned from the past few years of going through business is focus is key.

137
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:42,000
And for Jeremy right now, and for the business as well, YouTube long form is the highest ROI thing that we can do.

138
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:49,600
And we just want to focus on doubling down on not only increasing the output, but also the quality of the content that we put out.

139
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:59,840
That said, we are working on a few other projects where someone, you know, maybe a third party or another person that we hire can do independently.

140
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:09,440
So for example, we just onboarded a few clippers who are taking some of our existing long form and posting that across Instagram reels and TikToks.

141
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:22,240
And that's actually been quite helpful because when they're posting, some of these videos will go viral and we're able to see what sort of hooks that they use in those videos to inform some of our future videos.

142
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:25,840
So it's kind of been a two-way feedbackack loop for us.

143
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:28,240
And then how does that play into ads?

144
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:31,280
Because I know this is like one of the main topics I wanted to talk to you about today.

145
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:40,640
You all scaled up from nothing to $100,000 a month in ad spend and profitably and are really making a big push in that side of things.

146
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:45,520
So how how do you think about the pipeline of ad creation?

147
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,840
Are you using a ton of Jeremy's videos as a source for that or is it totally different?

148
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:51,280
Like, what does that look like?

149
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:51,920
Yeah.

150
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:57,840
So for us, actually, we're not relying on Jeremy at all for ads.

151
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:08,160
I mean, besides maybe a few pieces of old content that he's done, old short form content that he's done, we're really just trying to focus on how we can scale without relying on Jeremy.

152
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:21,040
And in addition to our goal of just expanding our reach, we wanted to kind of solve the bus problem where everything was reliant on Jeremy's YouTube channel for our growth.

153
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:28,560
And by focusing on meta and not relying on Jeremy, we're kind of building a new channel that can scale on its own.

154
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:33,680
So everything we've done from that end is mainly through like B-roll style videos.

155
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:36,560
We've used some testimonial content, a lot of statics now with.

156
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:37,360
AI.

157
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,560
So that's kind of the approach that we've taken with Meta.

158
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:42,160
That's fascinating.

159
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:49,360
And like, again, I would have expected and I actually didn't look through your ad library in preparation for this podcast.

160
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:58,880
So I should have known that coming in, but I would have guessed it would be way more Jeremy focused because he is that kind of celebrity YouTuber and there's so much opportunity to use him.

161
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:04,160
But it makes a ton of sense when, and this is an interesting part too for an influencer with an app.

162
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:11,840
If the main business of creating content on YouTube is so is very profitable and doing really well.

163
00:16:12,960 --> 00:16:16,480
that whole focus element of like how much time can you dedicate?

164
00:16:16,640 --> 00:16:31,680
And I even love the way you're saying it's like, you know, trying to make it so that they aren't the limiting factor on anything because, you know, as the kind of star of the channel, there's only so much content he can do, so much bandwidth he personally has and everything like that.

165
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:46,800
So it's interesting that and again, I think, you know, a lot of people who just assume like, oh yeah, you know, influencers like that you can just do video all day and everything, but it's it's a lot of work and a lot of mental energy and everything else.

166
00:16:46,960 --> 00:16:48,640
And so I love that you're all really trying to.

167
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,600
diversify away from him being the center of everything.

168
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:52,880
Don't get me wrong.

169
00:16:53,120 --> 00:17:00,240
I would love to get more of Jeremy's time to make some ads because I know they would perform quite well with the trust that he has.

170
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:03,440
But for him right now, he's already stretched to the max.

171
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:05,360
So yeah, it's all about focus for us.

172
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:05,840
Yeah.

173
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:26,480
And I mean, it's a really good perspective too for anyone pitching influencers to like really understand and internalize that the influencer you think, you know, is just sitting around or should be really excited about it's like even an influencer where it's their own app, you have to make really hard decisions about how to prioritize time.

174
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:31,760
So as I mentioned, you started from like nothing in scaled up meta this year.

175
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:32,320
Why meta?

176
00:17:33,040 --> 00:17:35,760
You know, talk me through the, the, that whole process too.

177
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:36,480
Yeah.

178
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:47,680
So we actually launched the app in early January 2025 and we didn't launch any meta ads until this year, January.

179
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:54,240
And the reason for that year gap was mainly because we wanted to double down on improving the product.

180
00:17:54,800 --> 00:18:22,000
We got a lot of feedback from our launch and we know from just looking at some of the numbers that as long as we're constantly improving our trial to paid and retention that lifts the floor for everything else and considering that our organic traffic is already converting at a healthy rate, we know that any paid traffic that we send will be much lower.

181
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:25,920
So we wanted to improve the floor for all traffic coming in.

182
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:29,920
So we spent that whole year just putting our heads down, improving the product.

183
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:36,400
And once we felt like the numbers were right in terms of our trial to paid or retention and our churn, then we.

184
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:41,040
Then we slowly started investing into these additional acquisition channels.

185
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:45,920
So I love that perspective that that product really is key.

186
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:56,240
And, and, you know, so many people think, you know, if I can just scale up on Meta, you know, if I just had $100,000 a month to blow in ads, of course I could be as successful.

187
00:18:56,480 --> 00:19:04,160
But, but the reality is like, it still really comes down to like people actually believing in the product, people using the product, people staying retained to the product.

188
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:05,680
Like that's how you build a great business.

189
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:16,000
Um, so how how how do you think about that part of the of the product versus growth balance of like how you put time into it, how you allocate resources and things.

190
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:18,160
Yeah, no, I 100% agree.

191
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:19,600
For us, product is everything.

192
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:26,400
A great product will lift the floor more than any growth hacker tactic will do.

193
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:28,640
So that's always been the core for us.

194
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:30,480
Yeah, that's amazing.

195
00:19:30,640 --> 00:19:34,400
So starting from zero this year, what's your experience been like scaling Meta?

196
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:37,040
What kind of attribution are you using?

197
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:40,880
How are you determining whether the spend is profitable?

198
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:49,600
Did you like everybody talks about, you know, burn fifty K just kind of learning the ropes or what was that process like going from zero to zero to 100?

199
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:51,920
Yeah, so there was a lot, for sure.

200
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:55,680
We've never tapped into meta before, let alone myself.

201
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:56,400
I mean, I had it.

202
00:19:56,640 --> 00:20:04,000
I have quite a bit of experience in marketing and copywriting, but the meta world was definitely new for us.

203
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:21,840
And I think one of the biggest things that we needed to figure out right away was just getting more data, especially when it came to trial to paid, because, like I mentioned earlier, organic, you have a certain trial to paid threshold or baseline because these users are generally a lot warmer.

204
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,320
Whereas trial to paid, you're essentially tapping.

205
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:29,520
Happing net new cold users who've never really seen the brand before.

206
00:20:30,160 --> 00:20:41,120
So we knew trial to paid was going to be lower, but we just needed to get a baseline of what that number would be in order for us to calculate how much we could actually spend to acquire a customer.

207
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:44,240
So that took about a month to do.

208
00:20:44,720 --> 00:21:00,640
And once we had a good set of numbers to work with, then it was all about testing different angles and different avatars who would resonate well with the product and how our product can uniquely solve their pain points.

209
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:10,640
So we had a lot of learnings from surveys from past customers coming in from warm traffic, and we pretty much just doubled down on finding specific avatars that work well.

210
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:19,120
And right now, we're in a process of constantly iterating on what's winning while also slowly adding on new, new avatars.

211
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:26,480
So we're just trying to create a proper iterative and testing loop that allows this engine to keep on running.

212
00:21:26,960 --> 00:21:27,040
Yeah.

213
00:21:27,280 --> 00:21:36,880
You mentioned earlier the trust side of things and how important trust is and the research that Jeremy's involved in that lends trust to the YouTube channel and to the app ultimately.

214
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:52,320
But how do you think about the creatives and kind of the way they shape the perspective of people coming into the app as far as like over promising or, you know, things that are going to get people to convert but then maybe quickly churn or whatever?

215
00:21:52,960 --> 00:22:13,200
That's a big reason why we decided to actually do meta in house rather than choose an agency is because we felt like we needed someone who really understood the product and was very passionate about the product and ultimately could promote the app in a way that isn't faking anything.

216
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:20,240
So we wanted to make sure that whatever we put out there actually relates with what the product actually does.

217
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:24,720
I'm sure you've probably seen a lot of companies that overpromise what we can actually deliver.

218
00:22:24,960 --> 00:22:32,880
And for us, we just wanted to make that experience from marketing to the product handoff as seamless as possible.

219
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:49,200
Have you seen certain ads perform poorly based on that hypothesis or have you sometimes seen the opposite where you see an ad really take off and then you're kind of like, oh, it's doing really well, but I'm not sure that's the message we want to to send?

220
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:50,560
Yeah, definitely.

221
00:22:50,880 --> 00:23:22,400
We've tested a lot of ads that are more like, I guess, click beat style or encourage a click and encourage someone to start a trial, but we quickly, like we can quickly see from the trial to paid that this is not the user that resonates the best with the app, so we'll see a huge drop in trial to paid, whereas maybe we think a lot deeper about who this person we're trying to target is, what their pain points are, and then how the app uniquely solves those pain points that brings in a much higher quality of traffic.

222
00:23:22,720 --> 00:23:30,960
So it's definitely a balance of, okay, we want people to start the trial at the cheapest cost, but also we don't want just anyone to start the trial.

223
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:32,720
What are you targeting against?

224
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:38,640
And what kind of data are you sending back to Facebook or Meta for the targeting?

225
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:56,480
Yeah, so for the targeting, it's we have a custom, one of our engineers built a custom dashboard where basically we can see and validate that every single new trial is coming specifically from Meta just because with all our organic reach right now, Meta can often blend who's coming from where.

226
00:23:57,040 --> 00:24:00,240
So we have that specifically set up for just meta conversions.

227
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:10,240
And basically, whenever a trial comes in, we feed the data, we're able to see the trial to paid numbers, and we're feeding the highest intent users back to Meta.

228
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:11,520
Gotcha.

229
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:20,480
And you read a post earlier, I think earlier this year, the biggest unlock to scaling your Meta ads, spend less time in Ads Manager.

230
00:24:20,880 --> 00:24:21,600
What did you mean by that?

231
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:28,080
And then how do you balance that against what you just said about you do still want to have like the right targeting and the right data coming back and everything?

232
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:29,840
Yeah, no, that's a great question.

233
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:32,000
I think that must have been on Twitter.

234
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:38,240
I recently started using Twitter and I just share any thoughts that come to my mind., kind of like a personal diary.

235
00:24:38,800 --> 00:24:49,760
But I think my point with that was it's super easy to get obsessed with setting up the right campaign, setting up all these different bid targeting strategies and all that.

236
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:57,600
But at the end of the day, I think what will really move the needle is just creating better content, same as on YouTube.

237
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:12,320
As long as you create content that people actually enjoy, then you're, you're going to make a much greater improvement than tinkering around with different targeting styles, especially now with, I'm not sure if you've heard of the whole Andromeda update, but a lot of the.

238
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:16,000
targeting on Meta is specifically done through the creatives.

239
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:27,440
So for me, that was just a reminder to focus on creating better creatives rather than tinkering around and mess around with the targeting that's really out of your control.

240
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:38,320
We've been talking through the scaling up on Meta, but one thing we haven't discussed is that you send most of your traffic to the web or all your traffic to the web, not to the app store.

241
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:39,360
Why do you do that?

242
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:40,080
Yeah.

243
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:50,800
So about 90% of our traffic is actually going to our website and we have a really long personalized quiz that we send all leads to on our website.

244
00:25:51,280 --> 00:26:03,120
The reason we do that is because it educates this quiz that we have, we've tested multiple times, but it educates the user on the actual product.

245
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:14,640
It overcomes objections and it increases the perceived value of the product while also helping them understand how personalized the product actually will be for them.

246
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:26,560
So for one, it increases the overall perceived value, but secondly, we actually use a lot of the questions in the quiz to build their personalized plan in the app.

247
00:26:26,960 --> 00:26:28,480
So it's kind of a two-fold.

248
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:34,000
And we've also tested the running traffic to the App Store versus the quiz.

249
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:45,760
And we've noticed that the quiz just has a much higher conversion, likely because some of the users right now who are going directly onto the App Store have a lot of a higher intent.

250
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,240
So maybe they don't need to take that quiz right away.

251
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:52,080
But down the road, it is something we're considering retesting.

252
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:58,960
You basically matched the quiz almost one for one in the app compared to the web, but you saw much better traffic on the web versus the app.

253
00:26:59,200 --> 00:26:59,520
Exactly.

254
00:26:59,680 --> 00:27:01,280
I mean, much better conversion., right.

255
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:28,800
I've talked to a few people about this recently, and one of the hypotheses around this is that when you open an app, you're kind of just trying to get to a solution quickly, like give me my workout plan, I'm going to go workout or whatever, or like, yeah, just more kind of solution focused, where on the web you're maybe a little more like researching and open to kind of reading a little more.

256
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:34,240
You're maybe not checking out at a grocery store or whatever where your attention is split.

257
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:35,920
Maybe even.

258
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:44,560
Do you even track like the difference between being on mobile versus being like in a desktop environment or anything like that?

259
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:45,280
Yeah.

260
00:27:45,680 --> 00:27:48,800
So the majority of our traffic is definitely on mobile.

261
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:51,600
So we're just tracking conversions separately.

262
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:57,760
And funny enough, though, we ran that same experiment with setting up the quiz on Android.

263
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:03,200
And that actually is converting equivalent to our conversions on the web.

264
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:05,920
It's just not the Apple Store users.

265
00:28:06,400 --> 00:28:07,840
Let's dive into pricing.

266
00:28:08,160 --> 00:28:10,320
And I love the Jim Buddy two-person plan.

267
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:11,360
So I'm curious about that.

268
00:28:11,680 --> 00:28:20,560
So what, what, what is your plan pricing and how do you think about pricing, especially with running ads and thinking about conversion?

269
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:29,120
Did you change your pricing once you started doing ads or had you dialed in your pricing and feel like that's actually been pretty effective once you started paying up ads?

270
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:32,720
So, yeah, so for pricing, we are slightly on the premium side.

271
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:37,040
So about 189 dollars for the year or 30 dollars a month.

272
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:43,920
And we actually just launched with that through when we launched the app back in 2025 through YouTube.

273
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:48,800
And we pretty much kept the pricing the exact same the entire time.

274
00:28:49,680 --> 00:29:03,840
And the reason for that is because the way we position our product is not as an app, but as a tracking app that most workout nutrition apps out there are, but it's more of like a personal trainer in your pocket.

275
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:11,760
So the positioning of it is you're comparing costs of a personal trainer versus a trainer in your pocket.

276
00:29:12,080 --> 00:29:14,400
So that way we can charge a little bit more premium.

277
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:19,200
Also, we do have that added benefit of all the trust that's been built through the YouTube.

278
00:29:19,840 --> 00:29:24,240
And when it comes to approaching Meta with this pricing, it's kind of the same thing.

279
00:29:25,120 --> 00:29:28,560
We don't mention that the app is a tracker at all.

280
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:32,080
It's more of a coach who's going to guide you on your journey.

281
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:36,880
So I think with that positioning, we're able to actually do well with this pricing.

282
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:37,360
Yeah.

283
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:49,360
You have one really cool pricing thing that I don't see a lot of people do is a gym buddy plan where you get two for you bundle two together almost like a family plan, but a gym buddy plan.

284
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:50,960
How do you do that?

285
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:51,680
Yeah.

286
00:29:52,000 --> 00:30:02,160
So this was actually an idea brought up by one of our product managers, but we got a lot of requests from existing members saying, hey, do you guys have a family plan?

287
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:04,000
I want to bring my husband, my wife.

288
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:09,040
And this came up often enough that, okay, maybe we wanted to test something.

289
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:22,400
So what we simply did was on our web quiz, we offered users the option to add a gym buddy, we call it, to their plan and get their plan at a discounted rate.

290
00:30:22,800 --> 00:30:25,520
And that actually has been quite successful for us.

291
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:31,200
Around 15% of anyone who starts a trial will actually add on a gym buddy.

292
00:30:31,440 --> 00:30:36,160
So not only has that increased just overall AOV, but also we found that.

293
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:42,960
that when someone does select a gym buddy, then they're more likely to stay on the app.

294
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:45,280
So that's also increased some of our attention.

295
00:30:45,760 --> 00:30:46,640
Yeah, that's fascinating.

296
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:57,760
I was talking to Life 360 about this, and that's one of the things they said was that their highest retaining cohorts are the, are the family units, and that's very much a family app, but they're for them.

297
00:30:58,400 --> 00:31:17,200
And I imagine for a lot of folks, if you can get multiple people committed, and then it's a little bit like, well, maybe one's using it and the other isn't, but then you don't want to cancel because and don't want to switch to the single plan because, well, I'll start working out in the future or whatever, a little bit like a gym membership maybe.

298
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,760
But how much are y'all actually discounting to kind of incentivize that?

299
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:23,520
Just 15%.

300
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:25,600
Both users will get 15% off.

301
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:27,840
Yeah, so it's not like a massive incentive.

302
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:33,760
So you're, I mean, that's pretty amazing if 15% of your users are selecting that.

303
00:31:33,920 --> 00:31:38,320
And so you're almost doubling your LTV per conversion.

304
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:39,520
Yep, exactly.

305
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:40,080
I mean.

306
00:31:40,880 --> 00:31:48,560
When you think about it too, there aren't many workout related apps either that offer the ability to work.

307
00:31:48,560 --> 00:31:52,800
to, you know, work out with a friend or connect in that way, such as like Strava might do.

308
00:31:53,280 --> 00:32:08,640
So not only from a growth perspective has this been valuable, but it's also helped us think more about strategy when it comes to the product and how we can maybe double down on this idea of creating more of a community within the app.

309
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:10,560
Does the app do that currently?

310
00:32:10,720 --> 00:32:13,840
So once you add a gym buddy, do you see their workouts?

311
00:32:14,160 --> 00:32:17,840
Do you like what aspects of community have you already brought into the app?

312
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:18,400
Yes.

313
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:30,480
So we actually just rolled this out, but for gym buddies right now, you're able to see what your partner., see when your partner works out, see what your partner eats, and you can send them a few nudges here and there.

314
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:32,800
So there's that collaboration in there.

315
00:32:33,360 --> 00:32:34,000
That's awesome.

316
00:32:34,080 --> 00:32:48,560
I don't I don't think that would work for my wife and I, but I could see I'm actually in a group, group text with my two brothers, and we we kind of hassle each other and you know, talk about like our fitness goals and stuff like that.

317
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:55,040
So I should I should get them on a Jim Buddy plan and we'll move our text thread into the app.

318
00:32:55,760 --> 00:32:57,040
You see what they eat every day.

319
00:32:57,600 --> 00:32:57,680
Yeah.

320
00:33:00,240 --> 00:33:00,880
That's awesome.

321
00:33:01,760 --> 00:33:01,840
Yeah.

322
00:33:02,160 --> 00:33:04,240
And what do you think about that premium pricing?

323
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:08,320
I mean, you mentioned, you know, $189 a year is a lot.

324
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:10,240
Do you feel like that's a barrier?

325
00:33:10,480 --> 00:33:21,440
Have you done any price testing or are you considering doing some price testing to see if lowering that barrier does improve things or has conversion been good enough that like it's not you don't feel like it's super low hanging fruit right now?

326
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:22,160
Yeah.

327
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:26,560
So I think for us right now, it just hasn't been a focus.

328
00:33:27,040 --> 00:33:35,920
We've been trying to double down on just improving the metrics given our current pricing and maybe down the road we'll experiment with pricing.

329
00:33:37,120 --> 00:33:44,880
can have a major impact, but a lot of it right now is when you do change pricing, you can influence a lot of the product metrics.

330
00:33:45,200 --> 00:33:50,240
So we'd rather stick with, okay, let's focus on improving retention and trial to paid.

331
00:33:50,880 --> 00:33:56,720
And once we're at a point where we feel like that's healthy, then we're going to start experimenting on these other things.

332
00:33:57,520 --> 00:34:09,680
With that high of a price and having looked probably at a state of subscription apps report, are you in the ballpark of the median or higher than the median on conversion with that high of a price?

333
00:34:10,400 --> 00:34:10,480
Yes.

334
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:24,640
So for our warm traffic., our organic traffic coming from YouTube, we are around the 35-40% range and trial to trial to paid for meta is slightly lower around 25%.

335
00:34:25,360 --> 00:34:30,960
Gotcha, which is super impressive at $190 a year.

336
00:34:31,680 --> 00:34:37,520
And what percentage of that does jump all the way to the annual versus like what percentage end up picking the monthly plan?

337
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:43,040
So right now it's about 75-80% choose the annual.

338
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:44,000
Wow.

339
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:51,120
And that was actually part of a recent experiment we did on the pricing page, which was a big win for us.

340
00:34:51,840 --> 00:34:51,920
Yeah.

341
00:34:53,120 --> 00:35:04,160
And speaking of the pricing page and going back to the gym buddy thing, so you asked that question in onboarding so that you don't have to confuse the paywall for people who don't have a gym buddy.

342
00:35:04,320 --> 00:35:09,200
So like a person who doesn't get a gym buddy doesn't even ever see that they can get that discount.

343
00:35:10,560 --> 00:35:14,640
So your pricing page is very focused on just the annual and monthly, right?

344
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:15,760
Correct.

345
00:35:16,240 --> 00:35:20,560
Well, on the quiz actually is where we ask, hey, do you have a gym buddy?

346
00:35:20,640 --> 00:35:22,000
Would you like to add them onto your plan?

347
00:35:22,560 --> 00:35:30,880
They can say yes, they can say no, but if they say no., on the pricing page, we actually have just a really small toggle that says a little bit about the Jim Buddy.

348
00:35:31,120 --> 00:35:33,920
So they have that option as well if they opted out in the quiz.

349
00:35:34,160 --> 00:35:41,600
Yeah, because I mean, I could see it becoming one of those like paywalls that just has way too many options and and and makes it confusing.

350
00:35:41,760 --> 00:35:47,440
So it makes sense to like ask it in the quiz and then not make it super prominent on the paywall itself.

351
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:58,640
We've been talking about metagrowth and then the YouTube channel as a base, but I know you've also been working on SEO and now AEO and getting more and more people saying that ChatGPT sent them to the app.

352
00:35:59,680 --> 00:36:00,320
How's that going?

353
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:01,600
It's been good.

354
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:08,080
It's an investment that we decided to do earlier this year as another channel for us to expand on.

355
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:17,840
The reason why is because traditionally we've had really strong traffic coming in from SEO from all our old blog posts.

356
00:36:18,080 --> 00:36:24,320
So about thirty percent, thirty to forty percent of our traffic coming in was from SEO.

357
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:32,160
But ever since the rise of AI and people using AI search a lot more, we slowly started seeing that decrease.

358
00:36:32,960 --> 00:36:39,920
So we decided to invest in hiring an external consultant to help us with some of this AI search.

359
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:49,840
And basically what it is is we just optimize a lot of our existing content, but also we double down on creating online tools.

360
00:36:50,320 --> 00:37:09,280
So for example, a calorie calculator, a macro calculator, a bunch of these tools that users could use in the web that not only added value, for example, they're able to figure out what calories they need to eat per day to lose fat, but also seamlessly transition into the quiz.

361
00:37:09,920 --> 00:37:11,840
So that's been working really well for us.

362
00:37:12,320 --> 00:37:16,560
And also tools just tend to rank quite well with AI search.

363
00:37:16,880 --> 00:37:24,080
So that's something we're going to continue to double down on, although it does take a bit more time to actually see results just like SEO.

364
00:37:24,560 --> 00:37:27,520
It's something that we are going to keep on investing in.

365
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:38,640
And do you see a difference in intent and conversion from the tools versus the like blog posts or is it fairly similar or dependent on the blog post?

366
00:37:39,600 --> 00:37:46,560
I would say it's dependent on the tool and the blog post, but for the most part, the tools have a slightly higher conversion.

367
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:47,840
Interesting.

368
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:59,040
We've talked a bit about your thoughts on experimentation, but I did want to dig a little deeper into how you think about experimenting, what kind of experiments you've run, what kind of results you've seen.

369
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:01,600
So, how do you think about experimentation?

370
00:38:02,640 --> 00:38:13,440
Yeah, so I think one of the biggest shifts we've had over the last few years of experimentation is we're obsessed with the problem and not the solution.

371
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:24,960
And what I mean by that is rather than going ahead and just simply testing out ideas for experiments, we take our time to really truly understand what actually is a problem we're trying to solve.

372
00:38:25,840 --> 00:38:28,400
And the way we do that is like through three main steps.

373
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:30,560
So first, we'll look at the data.

374
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:37,120
We track everything on amplitude and we know where there's drop offs on our web, on the app onboarding.

375
00:38:37,760 --> 00:38:46,320
And we'll look at what certain areas have the biggest drop off, but also that we feel have the biggest area to improve.

376
00:38:46,720 --> 00:39:02,160
And once we have an idea of what we actually want to experiment on, for example, let's say it's a pricing page, then we'll go ahead and look at that pricing page and we'll list out as many problems as to why that pricing page isn't converting before anything else.

377
00:39:02,720 --> 00:39:05,360
So maybe it's a lack of trust on that page.

378
00:39:05,680 --> 00:39:07,840
Maybe the value proposition isn't clear.

379
00:39:08,160 --> 00:39:15,360
We'll list out as many problems as possible and as a team we'll decide on what we think the biggest problem is.

380
00:39:15,920 --> 00:39:20,640
And then only once we have that problem locked in, then we'll start listing out solutions.

381
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:24,080
So if it's trust, then maybe we need testimonials.

382
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:25,680
We need more credibility.

383
00:39:27,440 --> 00:39:31,520
based off of the solution, then we'll just go up and roll it and experiment.

384
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:34,640
So yeah, that's been the main process.

385
00:39:34,880 --> 00:40:13,840
And I think the biggest thing from all of that is making sure you document down everything just because it's it's it's it's easy to send a message to your engineer on slack and say hey like let's let's roll this out but once you actually take the time to write things down you understand the problem so much deeper and not only that but if an experiment fails you can go back to the drawing board and understand what exactly you tested why it failed and how you can iterate on it because there's been I mean probably 70 percent of the experiments that we run fail on the first go and that doesn't mean it's a failure.

386
00:40:14,640 --> 00:40:18,080
It just means that maybe there's something wrong with the execution.

387
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:19,600
Maybe we covered the wrong problem.

388
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:24,640
So often we're iterating on these failures to uncover a winner.

389
00:40:24,960 --> 00:40:27,040
So that's been a really helpful process for us.

390
00:40:27,600 --> 00:40:27,680
Yeah.

391
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:40,640
I really like that, that problem focused approach because, I mean, it's so much more kind of natural and easy in some ways to just, just brainstorm solutions like, oh, it's not converting well.

392
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:44,480
Let's make this prettier or let's add this testimonial or whatever.

393
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:52,560
But I love that approach of like really deeply thinking through all the potential problems first and then brainstorming solutions.

394
00:40:52,800 --> 00:41:10,960
And then it just gives you such a clearer understanding of what you're testing and then helps you interpret the results better because you had a hypothesis around exactly what the problem was, then you had a hypothesis around what would solve it and then you can test both sides of that equation instead of just saying it was a complete failure.

395
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:11,680
So I love that.

396
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:12,320
Exactly.

397
00:41:12,800 --> 00:41:13,600
And just to add on that.

398
00:41:13,840 --> 00:41:24,800
So when you do find a winner, for example, one thing that's been really impactful for us is taking those learnings and applying what worked to other parts of the funnel.

399
00:41:25,200 --> 00:41:28,960
So say for example a messaging really worked on our quiz.

400
00:41:29,360 --> 00:41:32,720
Maybe one of we want to test applying that on somewhere in the app.

401
00:41:33,200 --> 00:41:40,080
So taking those learnings and applying it across all points within your product and your web.

402
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:42,960
How granular do you get with testing?

403
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:45,280
I know this is kind of a controversial topic.

404
00:41:46,080 --> 00:41:53,120
Some people are like, Oh yeah, I changed my CTA button from yellow to blue and the conversion skyrocketed.

405
00:41:53,440 --> 00:41:57,040
Are you doing super granular experiments or are you testing like kind of bigger swings?

406
00:41:57,680 --> 00:41:59,840
For us, we're definitely testing bigger swings.

407
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:07,920
Again, it's about focus and we'd rather just focus on what we think based on the data can move the biggest needle.

408
00:42:08,320 --> 00:42:15,280
And I don't think for the most part it will be with changing colors and changing a small piece of text.

409
00:42:16,160 --> 00:42:16,240
Yeah.

410
00:42:16,480 --> 00:42:17,360
And I mean, it does work.

411
00:42:17,600 --> 00:42:21,440
It does work sometimes and maybe some people have seen it work and then get over indexed on that.

412
00:42:21,680 --> 00:42:24,640
But sometimes, those kind of things can move the needle.

413
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:27,200
Some little things can be dramatically.

414
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:40,400
I mean, especially when you, when it does come to like CTA texts and things like that, like the subtle aspects of how people are thinking about what action happens after they tap a button.

415
00:42:40,880 --> 00:42:48,720
Like I can see some of these like really high leverage points being able to test more granular stuff.

416
00:42:48,880 --> 00:42:55,680
But yeah, it's like if you can test a big swing and and and and try more dramatically new stuff, you just get clear results too.

417
00:42:56,240 --> 00:42:56,320
Yeah.

418
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:57,600
No, no, I agree.

419
00:42:57,840 --> 00:43:01,440
Not to say that a little text change won't make a difference.

420
00:43:01,680 --> 00:43:14,000
I think for us it's just been when we have a problem that we're trying to solve and we have these different hypotheses for why we'll usually test it as a batch just because again, it also depends on how much traffic you have coming in.

421
00:43:14,400 --> 00:43:19,200
We won't have enough to do a bunch of mini tests, so we'd rather focus on a bigger swing.

422
00:43:19,440 --> 00:43:25,520
If that doesn't work, okay, maybe we'll strip things down a little bit, but that's usually how we've been approaching the experimentation.

423
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:26,080
Yeah.

424
00:43:26,880 --> 00:43:48,320
And then because you, as you said earlier, have kind of looked through the funnel and kind of estimated the likely impact, you're also looking for those high leverage places anyway, like places where people fall off the funnel, places where you feel like conversion should be dramatically higher than it is and not testing as much the places where you're already seeing.

425
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:49,760
really good results.

426
00:43:50,160 --> 00:43:50,560
Exactly.

427
00:43:50,960 --> 00:43:51,040
Yeah.

428
00:43:51,280 --> 00:44:20,000
So it's like picking the test is leverage and then what you what you test is leverage or picking where to test and then what to test are both leverage points that it's easy to like, yeah, I want to test a million different things, but like realistically, even with AI and moving quicker, it's it's honestly more about traffic these days, it's like, can you like if it takes a week to get to statistical significance, that's a very different flow of experimentation than if it took an hour to get to statistical significance.

429
00:44:20,320 --> 00:44:22,240
You're not getting many as many at bats.

430
00:44:22,400 --> 00:44:26,240
So you gotta be really careful about where about where you take the swings, right?

431
00:44:26,720 --> 00:44:26,800
Yeah.

432
00:44:27,040 --> 00:44:33,280
I mean, if we had as much traffic as Netflix or Spotify, I think we would definitely be testing a lot more.

433
00:44:33,920 --> 00:44:34,000
Yeah.

434
00:44:34,560 --> 00:44:35,360
That makes sense.

